<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baptism of Jesus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The baptism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> is described in the three Synoptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>, Matthew, Mark and Luke, and is placed at Al-Maghtas (Bethany Beyond the Jordan), in present-day Jordan. In each, the Spirit descends on Jesus and a voice from Heaven speaks, after which the Gospels place his forty days in the Judean desert. Modern biblical scholars regard the baptism as a historical event assigned a high degree of certainty, and often start from it in studying the historical Jesus. Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> commemorates it on 6 January, the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> or Theophany, and Western churches recall it in the following week.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baptism-of-jesus.jpg" alt="Baptism of Jesus" width="330" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piero della Francesca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piero,_battesimo_di_cristo_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Luke places the start of the ministry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> in the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar. The traditional approach takes the reign to begin when Tiberius became co-regent in 11 CE, which puts John's ministry about 26 CE, while some scholars count from the death of Augustus in 14 CE, implying 29 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> gives the place as Bethany beyond the Jordan, generally taken to be on the eastern bank near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, though Eusebius of Caesarea put it on the west bank. UNESCO-sponsored excavations at Al-Maghtas on the east side found what has been deemed the earliest place of worship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In all three of Matthew, Mark and Luke the Spirit descends on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> immediately after his baptism and a voice speaks from Heaven. In Mark and Luke the voice addresses Jesus directly, while in Matthew it speaks of him in the third person. Matthew alone records a conversation in which John says he needs to be baptised by Jesus, until Jesus persuades him to do it 'to fulfil all righteousness'. Luke says Jesus was praying and that the Spirit descended in bodily form like a dove. After the baptism the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> describe Jesus fasting for forty days in the Judean desert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Nearly all scholars hold that John baptised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>; James Dunn states that this and the crucifixion command almost universal assent. Two arguments support it: the criterion of embarrassment, since John baptised for the remission of sins and the early Church would have had no reason to invent a story that put John above Jesus, and multiple attestation, since Acts also refers to the baptism John preached. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> also wrote of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, though he does not relate John to Jesus. Most Christian denominations treat the event as a basis for the rite of baptism, and Christian art typically shows the sky opening and the Holy Spirit descending as a dove.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baptism of Jesus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Earliest Christian art depicted the raising of Lazarus, the <b>baptism of Jesus</b> and the Good Shepherd.</blockquote><a href="/work/new-testament">New Testament · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
